\description{
Draw samples from the posterior distribution of an estimated model
}
\details{
The \code{lm}, \code{glm}, \code{glmmTMB}, \code{GJRM::gamlss}, \code{sdmTMB}, and full mixed-model
diagnostic samplers currently use the fitting observations only. They draw
coefficients together using Gaussian approximations. See
\code{\link[=posterior_predictive_plot]{posterior_predictive_plot()}} for which additional distribution parameters
remain fixed and how random effects are treated for each fitter.

With no explicit \code{data}, observation rows follow the fitted model's
\code{na.action}. \code{na.exclude} restores excluded rows as \code{NA} in every draw;
\code{na.omit} returns retained rows only. \code{.row} indexes the fitting data after
any \code{subset}. With explicit \code{data}, \code{.row} indexes that data, missing
responses do not prevent prediction, and missing required predictors give
\code{NA} draws. An explicit prediction \code{na.action} in \code{...} is honoured without
renumbering retained rows. Restoration does not consume random numbers.

For Gaussian identity-link \code{bam} and \code{scam} fits, first-order autoregressive (AR1) errors
make consecutive responses correlated. Responses are drawn together to retain
this fitted correlation, restarting at each \code{AR.start} segment. These draws
currently use the fitting observations only. For other families or links,
the AR1 correction applies to working residuals used during fitting. It does
not specify a joint distribution of actual responses, so sampling produces
an error.

Location-scale-shape families \code{\link[mgcv:gaulss]{mgcv::gaulss()}}, \code{\link[mgcv:gammals]{mgcv::gammals()}},
\code{\link[mgcv:gumbls]{mgcv::gumbls()}}, \code{\link[mgcv:gevlss]{mgcv::gevlss()}}, \code{\link[mgcv:shash]{mgcv::shash()}}, \code{\link[mgcv:twlss]{mgcv::twlss()}} and
\code{\link[mgcv:ziplss]{mgcv::ziplss()}} support fitting observations, subsets and new covariates.
Every distribution parameter is computed from the same joint coefficient
draw. Formula offsets and prescribed smooth contributions are retained.
Evaluation data need predictors and formula-offset variables, but no
observed response. mgcv natively represents the second \code{gaulss()} parameter
as reciprocal standard deviation: response sampling uses SD, divided by the square
root of the sampling weight. Other LSS generators currently ignore sampling
weights, following their mgcv implementations. Parameters represented by
coefficients vary jointly; other family parameters remain fixed.

Cox survival models require evaluation times and an observation process and
do not have a response generator for this interface. Censored-family draws
are latent, uncensored responses; reproducing observed censoring requires
an observation process and remains unsupported in posterior predictive checks.
Grouped-family posterior response draws support fitting observations and
supplied data with known family membership. Component nuisance parameters
remain fixed. \code{.parameter} identifies the component used for each row.
Censored grouped components require an observation process; use \code{simulate()}
for their latent, uncensored responses.

With \code{marginalise}, selected random effects are drawn for new groups in
each response replicate. Group labels must be supplied for new data.
See \code{\link[=random_effect_samples]{random_effect_samples()}} for the estimated Gaussian distributions.
}
\section{Note}{
With \code{data = NULL}, fitting offsets are retained, including offsets
supplied through the fitting function's \code{offset} argument. Explicit prediction
data follow \code{\link[mgcv:predict.gam]{mgcv::predict.gam()}}: offsets in formulas are evaluated from
the prediction data, while offsets supplied through the fitting function's
\code{offset} argument are not transferred to new observations. Include offsets
in the formula when predicting at new data; the required offset variables
must then be present in \code{data}.
}

